Case search result (read-only, nothing built/posted). Plain answer: I found NO sourced case of someone OCR'ing hundreds of pages with ORT+DirectML on PP-OCR, hitting slow warm-up/per-page speed, and posting "I changed X, now N pages/min". Several searches (RapidOCR, PaddleOCR-ONNX, OCRmyPDF plugins, docTR/OnnxTR, ORT DML issues) gave only the adjacent cases below. Anything I say beyond them is inference.

Closest concrete cases (each sourced):
1. Same root cause on DirectML, fixed by removing dynamic axes. Whisper decoder: DML 250 ms vs CUDA 50 ms; "the solution was to remove the dynamic axis", which brought DML to 50 ms. https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/issues/14550 (mirrored at https://github.com/microsoft/Windows-Machine-Learning/issues/513). Also https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/issues/6978 (dynamic shape 8x dynamic x100 about 10x slower than fixed 8x1000x100 on GPU; thread shows the symptom, no PP-OCR). Official guidance for this exact fix (edit model to fixed dims, or AddFreeDimensionOverrideByName, or denotations): https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/execution-providers/DirectML-ExecutionProvider.html. Not OCR and not hundreds of pages, but the closest "dynamic vs fixed on DML" evidence, same ORT.
2. A PP-OCR-on-DirectML timing, in the other direction: RapidOCR user, 60 line images 1180x92, PP-OCRv5 mobile det/rec/cls via RapidOCRSharpOnnx, ORT 1.24.4: CUDA provider 63.4 s vs DirectML 11.1 s (the report is about CUDA being slow on the dynamic model; DML was the "normal speed" one). https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/issues/28305. Useful as a proof PP-OCR works under DML, and as a data point that dynamic-shape PP-OCR cost is provider-dependent; no fix or per-page figure given; 60 lines in 11 s is slow (about 0.18 s/line), no hardware tuning shown beyond RTX 5090 + Core Ultra 9 285K.
3. Windows OCR pipeline tuning with PP-OCR ONNX + optional DirectML (Rust, oar-ocr), merged PR: https://github.com/GreatV/oar-ocr/pull/165. What they changed: device- and model-aware batch sizes (large outer batches were hurting on CPU because ORT already multithreads per op, so tensors/memory traffic grew; accelerators keep throughput defaults), removed per-crop buffer copies in recognition preprocessing, ORT global thread pool. Measured on Windows 11, Ryzen 9 7950X (CPU): 8 small pages 1,991 ms -> 1,161 ms; single medium page 894 ms -> 487 ms; 4 dense pages 2,040 -> 1,846 ms. So tuned CPU PP-OCR runs roughly 0.15-0.5 s per page on a fast desktop CPU for their test pages (not a book of dense text, and the 7950X is a top CPU). Relevant to his CPU worry and to our preprocessing cost; it also says: do NOT assume bigger batch is better on CPU.
4. Whole-PDF batching in an OCRmyPDF plugin: PaddleOCR plugin with "one model per process, batched page inference", user-set --paddle-batch-size (example 2), jobs, thread overrides. https://pypi.org/project/ocrmypdf-paddleocr-plus/. Shows batch tuning is the knob people expose; no throughput numbers in the README.
5. RapidOCR sources on shapes/batching: the maintainers fixed the rec input to [3,48,320] ("tested best", older pypi doc https://pypi.org/project/rapidocr-onnxruntime/1.0.1/) and describe sorting lines by aspect ratio and batching to cut padding (https://deepwiki.com/rapidai/rapidocr/3.3-text-recognition, https://deepwiki.com/rapidai/rapidocr/8.3-performance-optimization; deepwiki is generated docs, treat as secondary). Detector cost: one user measured RapidOCR DET 101-210 ms vs PaddleOCR 48 ms per frame (rec 20-30 ms), i.e. our-style ORT detection pre/post-processing matters (https://github.com/RapidAI/RapidOCR/issues/514). Nothing about DirectML warm-up.
6. docTR CPU (not ORT/PP-OCR): 1.6-10 s per scanned page on a laptop; maintainer reply: 0.8 s/page on an i7-11800H using a lighter det/rec pair and batch inference (det_bs=4, rec_bs=256) https://github.com/mindee/doctr/discussions/876. A weak signal that CPU OCR of ~1 s/page is normal on a laptop with small models, but that is PyTorch docTR.
7. Windows ML compile-caching guidance (apps must cache compiled models or users pay every start; this is Windows ML EP compile, not DML in plain ORT): https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/ai/new-windows-ml/model-compilation. Checked ORT EP context docs; I found no statement that the DML EP supports persisting compiled graphs (inference: it doesn't, so a long-lived worker is our cache).

What this means (inference, not sourced beyond the above): the strongest evidence-backed lever is the one already proposed (real fixed shapes via dimension overrides, small number of sessions, batching tuned per device). Evidence that CPU is much better than he fears is moderate: tuned CPU PP-OCR at 0.15-0.5 s/page on a fast desktop (case 3) versus his GPU 7-10 s, though his book pages are denser than their test pages and his CPU model is unknown. Hence the CPU benchmark line is the cheapest way to settle it, and I would run it on his PC before choosing a default. I could also use his measured data: the OLD build on his machine did normal pages in 0.5-0.9 s and dense ones in ~5 s with DML warm shapes, which beats every sourced figure above, so the achievable GPU target for this model on his box is the old speed.
EOF
